#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111526} :activity/id #uuid "224e6883-ecf1-58d2-8217-7503c15c1910" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049846 :topic/id #uuid "1843810f-c12f-5f01-b69f-9484dfe1a88e" :topic/math-academy-id 1413} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111911 :tutorial/content "If we have rational expressions on both sides of an equation, say,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{red}x-1}}{{\\color{blue}2}} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}2x-1}}{{\\color{red}5}}\n$$\n\nthen we can eliminate the rational expressions by applying **cross-multiplication**:\n\n- we cross-multiply the left-hand side numerator $({\\color{red}x-1})$ with the right-hand side denominator $({\\color{red}5})$, and\n- we cross-multiply the left-hand side denominator $({\\color{blue}2})$ with the right-hand side numerator $({\\color{blue}2x-1})$.\n\nDoing this, we get the following equation:\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}x-1}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}5}) = ({\\color{blue}2}) \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2x - 1})\n$$\n\nAfter we've cross-multiplied, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 1) \\cdot 5 &= 2 \\cdot (2x - 1) \\\\\n5x - 5 &= 4x - 2 \\\\\n5x - 5 + 5 &= 4x - 2 + 5 \\\\\n5x &= 4x + 3 \\\\\n5x - 4x &= 4x + 3 - 4x \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Cross-multiplication might seem magical, but it is really just a shortcut for multiplying both sides of the equation by each denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x - 1}{2} &= \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{x - 1}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{x - 1}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\\\\nx - 1 &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\\\\n(x - 1) \\cdot 5 &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n(x - 1) \\cdot 5 &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2x - 1}{5} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n(x - 1) \\cdot (5) &= (2) \\cdot (2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "99ff1453-e12b-518f-94b1-9baf8ee914e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8174 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "62154097-0663-58a8-8dfe-b1f260a5583e" :step/math-academy-id 175966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110619 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110320 :question/id #uuid "7df7a073-8131-5732-b353-f89484052e38" :question/math-academy-id "e-13077"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff2f965-b406-5c9a-bd41-73ccdce951eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048760}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111182}] :knowledge-point/title "Cross-Multiplying with One Binomial"} :step/id #uuid "79e64205-d43a-5719-a973-65d5eb873b04" :step/math-academy-id 175967 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111528}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110620 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110321 :question/id #uuid "4041cf6f-61b8-5794-9988-436722985bcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-13078"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e880edeb-82ad-5943-a846-dc3246e3e3bd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050908}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111155}] :knowledge-point/title "Cross-Multiplying with Two Binomials"} :step/id #uuid "39ea7244-0ca1-52cc-9e51-81973115c977" :step/math-academy-id 175971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111912 :tutorial/content "Consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{2} = -\\dfrac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nEven though one of the fractions is negative, we can still use cross-multiplication. All we have to do is transfer the negative into the numerator of the fraction, and proceed with cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x + 1}{2} &= -\\frac{x}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{x + 1}{2} &= \\frac{-x}{3} \\\\\n(x + 1) \\cdot 3 &= 2 \\cdot (-x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1) \\cdot 3 &= 2 \\cdot (-x) \\\\\n3x + 3 &= -2x \\\\\n3x + 3 - 3x &= -2x - 3x \\\\\n3 &= -5x \\\\\n\\frac{3}{-5} &= \\frac{-5x}{-5} \\\\\n- \\frac{3}{5} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ed67f4e3-4ff1-5900-bda0-c7f4e2b5a652" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8175 :tutorial/title "Cross-Multiplying with a Negative Sign"} :step/id #uuid "a5826411-42a0-5ce0-aa7f-6e0d021d40fa" :step/math-academy-id 175974 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110621 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110322 :question/id #uuid "d97adcd5-65ea-5cf7-905a-db954e43904b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13079"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59230000-ef0e-56c0-ab06-17676d817d5a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049939}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234052}] :knowledge-point/title "Cross-Multiplying with a Negative Sign"} :step/id #uuid "1f42768c-0adb-5fc8-8dd3-b63229bed312" :step/math-academy-id 175975}] :activity/title "Solving Linear Equations Using Cross-Multiplication" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110809}]}
